      "text": "1. **Setting up the system of equations:**\n   We start with the given equations:\n   \\[\n   x + y = 4\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   3xy = 4 \\implies xy = \\frac{4}{3}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Expressing \\( y \\) in terms of \\( x \\):**\n   From the first equation:\n   \\[\n   y = 4 - x\n   \\]\n   Substitute \\( y = 4 - x \\) into the equation \\( xy = \\frac{4}{3} \\):\n   \\[\n   x(4 - x) = \\frac{4}{3}\n   \\]\n   Simplify this to form a quadratic equation:\n   \\[\n   4x - x^2 = \\frac{4}{3}\n   \\]\n   Multiply through by 3 to clear the fraction:\n   \\[\n   12x - 3x^2 = 4\n   \\]\n   Rearrange into standard quadratic form:\n   \\[\n   3x^2 - 12x + 4 = 0\n   \\]\n\n3. **Solving the quadratic equation:**\n   Use the quadratic formula \\( x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\) where \\( a = 3 \\), \\( b = -12 \\), and \\( c = 4 \\):\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{12 \\pm \\sqrt{(-12)^2 - 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4}}{2 \\cdot 3}\n   \\]\n   Simplify under the square root:\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{12 \\pm \\sqrt{144 - 48}}{6}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{12 \\pm \\sqrt{96}}{6}\n   \\]\n   Simplify \\(\\sqrt{96}\\):\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt{96} = \\sqrt{16 \\cdot 6} = 4\\sqrt{6}\n   \\]\n   So,\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{12 \\pm 4\\sqrt{6}}{6} = \\frac{12}{6} \\pm \\frac{4\\sqrt{6}}{6} = 2 \\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the solutions for \\( x \\) are:\n   \\[\n   x = 2 + \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad x = 2 - \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Forming the expressions:**\n   The solutions for \\( x \\) are in the form:\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{a \\pm b\\sqrt{c}}{d}\n   \\]\n   Here, \\( a = 6 \\), \\( b = 2 \\), \\( c = 6 \\), and \\( d = 3 \\).\n\n5. **Calculating \\( a + b + c + d \\):**\n   \\[\n   a + b + c + d = 6 + 2 + 6 + 3 = 17\n   \\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{17}\n\\]",
